# LuneOS IPv4 host firewall, stateless fallback.
#
# luneos-devmode-firewall ("load 4", run by iptables.service) loads this
# only when iptables.rules cannot be loaded, which in practice means the
# kernel has no connection tracking (nf_conntrack/xt_conntrack missing or
# blacklisted): iptables-restore then rejects iptables.rules as a whole.
# Before this file existed that left every policy at ACCEPT. This file
# uses no conntrack match, so it still fails closed.
#
# It admits the same new connections as iptables.rules: loopback, the USB
# gadget's DHCP, SSH and adb, DHCP and DNS on connman's "tether" bridge,
# mDNS, ping, and SSH on Wi-Fi when developer mode puts it in
# LUNEOS-DEVMODE.
#
# Replies to the device's own connections cannot be told apart from new
# packets without conntrack, so they are let in by shape instead:
# - TCP: every segment without SYN alone (`! --syn`). An outside host can
#   still send ACK/FIN/RST segments to any port, which allows ACK scans and
#   a little fingerprinting, but it cannot open a connection: that needs a
#   bare SYN, which is dropped unless a rule above lets it in.
# - UDP: only DNS (source port 53) and NTP (source port 123) answers, to
#   unprivileged ports (and 123 to 123). A sender can fake those source
#   ports, so any UDP service on a port >= 1024 is reachable from port 53
#   or 123. Other UDP replies (QUIC/HTTP3, STUN, VoIP/SIP, VPNs) are
#   dropped; those applications fall back to TCP or fail.
# - Tethering does not work: connman's NAT needs conntrack anyway, so
#   FORWARD drops everything here.
*filter
:INPUT DROP [0:0]
:FORWARD DROP [0:0]
:OUTPUT ACCEPT [0:0]
:LUNEOS-DEVMODE - [0:0]

-A INPUT -i lo -j ACCEPT

# Replies, stateless (see above).
-A INPUT -p tcp ! --syn -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -p udp --sport 53 --dport 1024:65535 -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -p udp --sport 123 --dport 1024:65535 -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -p udp --sport 123 --dport 123 -j ACCEPT

# ICMP: ping both ways and the errors path MTU discovery needs.
-A INPUT -p icmp --icmp-type echo-request -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -p icmp --icmp-type echo-reply -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -p icmp --icmp-type destination-unreachable -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -p icmp --icmp-type time-exceeded -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -p icmp --icmp-type parameter-problem -j ACCEPT

# DHCP client (connman).
-A INPUT -p udp --sport 67 --dport 68 -j ACCEPT

# mDNS (avahi-daemon).
-A INPUT -p udp --dport 5353 -j ACCEPT

# USB gadget network, as in iptables.rules.
-A INPUT -i usb+ -p udp --dport 67 -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -i rndis+ -p udp --dport 67 -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -i usb+ -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -i rndis+ -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -i usb+ -p tcp --dport 5555 -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -i rndis+ -p tcp --dport 5555 -j ACCEPT

# connman tethering: DHCP and DNS for the clients on "tether" (they get no
# further than the device itself; see above).
-A INPUT -i tether -p udp --dport 67 -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -i tether -p udp --dport 53 -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -i tether -p tcp --dport 53 -j ACCEPT

# Developer mode's own rules (luneos-devmode-firewall).
-A INPUT -j LUNEOS-DEVMODE
COMMIT
